#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hypergeometric_u

#% text
# `hypergeometric_u(a, b, z)` gives Tricomi's confluent hypergeometric function `U(a, b, z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `U(a, b, z)` is the solution of Kummer's equation that behaves like `z^(-a)` as `z` tends to `inf` (DLMF 13.2.6). It has a branch cut on the negative real axis.

#% item
# Real machine numbers and balls give numerical values. `hypergeometric_u` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(1, 2, 1.0)
#% output
#= 1.0

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(0.5, 1.5, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.7071067811865476

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(1.0, 1.0, 10.0)
#% output
#= 0.09156333393978808

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several points:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(0.5, 1.5, [0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [1.4142135623730951, 1.0, 0.7071067811865476]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `U(1/2, 1/2, x^2)` is `sqrt(pi)*exp(x^2)*erfc(x)`:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(0.5, 0.5, 1.0) - sqrt(pi)*exp(1.0)*erfc(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.1102230246251565e-16

#% text
# The large-argument behaviour `z^(-a)`:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(0.7, 1.3, 1000.0)*1000.0^0.7
#% output
#= 0.9997203324825422

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A complex argument is evaluated by Tricomi's formula (DLMF 13.2.42) with the principal power of `z`; on the negative real axis that is the value from above the cut:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(1.0, 2.0, 1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.5 - 0.5im

#% text
# A positive integer `b` is the logarithmic case, evaluated as the mean of the two sides `b + eps` and `b - eps`; `U(1, 3, z) = 1/z + 1/z^2`:

#% cell
[hypergeometric_u(1, 3, 3.), 4/9]
#% output
#= [0.4444444444444444, 4/9]

#% text
# At 30 digits the cancellation of the two sides is absorbed in the working precision:

#% cell
n(hypergeometric_u(5, 3, 1/100), 30)
#% output
#= 405.0470588768353743722841360664760


#% text
# At a non-positive integer a, a polynomial:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(-2, b, z)
#% output
#= b - 2*z - 2*b*z + b^2 + z^2

#% text
# Its value there:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(-2, 1, 1.5)
#% output
#= -1.75

#% text
# Complex arguments:

#% cell
hypergeometric_u(3 - im, 2*im, 3.)
#% output
#= -0.0052653933206882 + 0.0007931661643468159im

#% text
# The derivative in z:

#% cell
d(hypergeometric_u(a, b, x), x)
#% output
#= -a*hypergeometric_u(1 + a, 1 + b, x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hypergeometric_1f1`, `whittaker_w`, `exp_integral_e`
